<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kazembe</h1><p class="st-meta">1740 CE – 1899 CE (approx.) · capital Mwansabombwe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern Lunda kingdom of the Luapula valley, a wealthy trading state visited by Portuguese explorers and David Livingstone.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1740 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> general sent east by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwata-yamvo">Mwata Yamvo</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luapula-river">Luapula</a> valley south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-mweru">Lake Mweru</a>. His successors, bearing the title Mwata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazembe">Kazembe</a>, built an independent kingdom that still paid tribute to the Lunda court.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazembe">Kazembe</a> ruled from a large capital through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> aristocrats who governed local chiefs. The kingdom traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves with Portuguese from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tete">Tete</a> and Swahili-Arab merchants from the east coast. The Portuguese explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-lacerda-e-almeida">Francisco de Lacerda</a> died there in 1798, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">Livingstone</a> visited in 1867–68. The annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutomboko">Mutomboko ceremony</a> commemorates the conquest.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the late 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/msiri">Msiri's</a> Yeke state took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> and Swahili-Arab traders eroded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazembe">Kazembe's</a> power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> forces occupied the capital in 1899, and the kingdom was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>. The Mwata Kazembe remains a traditional ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kazembe.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kazembe" width="320" height="424"><figcaption>Image: ChaloNiZambia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mwata_Kazembe_at_Mtomboko_2017_01.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1740 CE – 1899 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mwansabombwe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kazembe III Lukwesa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Lunda religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lunda, Bemba</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a>, <a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lunda of Kazembe, Mwata Kazembe, Eastern Lunda</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1820&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kazembe" aria-label="Kingdom of Kazembe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5402 2715 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Kazembe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5662 2807h0M5863 2875h0M5743 2822h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5722 2971h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5722 2971h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Zambia. <a href="/atlas?year=1820&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kazembe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

